{"name":"Ebony Clay","hex":"#323438","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ebony","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hard, dense, deep black wood from various subtropical and tropical trees, especially of the genus Diospyros.","A tree that yields such wood."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of ebony wood.","A deep, dark black colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ebony"},{"term":"clay","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mineral substance made up of small crystals of silica and alumina, that is ductile when moist; the material of pre-fired ceramics.","An earth material with ductile qualities."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To add clay to or to spread clay onto.","To purify using clay."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/clay"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ebony","blocks":[{"text":"Ebony is a dense black–brown hardwood, coming from several species in the genus Diospyros, which also includes the persimmon. A few Diospyros species, such as macassar and mun ebony, are dense enough to sink in water. Ebony is finely textured and has a mirror finish when polished, making it valuable as an ornamental wood. It is often cited as one of the most expensive woods in the world."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/%C3%89bano.jpg/330px-%C3%89bano.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ebony","wikidata":"Q156005"},{"title":"Clay","blocks":[{"text":"Clay is a type of fine-grained natural soil material containing clay minerals. Most pure clay minerals are white or light-coloured, but natural clays show a variety of colours from impurities, such as a reddish or brownish colour from small amounts of iron oxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Boulder_clay_cliff_south_of_Withernsea_%28geograph_6054962%29.jpg/330px-Boulder_clay_cliff_south_of_Withernsea_%28geograph_6054962%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clay","wikidata":"Q42302"},{"title":"Shades of black","blocks":[{"text":"Shades of black, or off-black colors, are colors that differ only slightly from pure black. These colors have a low lightness. From a photometric point of view, a color which differs slightly from black always has low relative luminance. Colors often considered \"shades of black\" include onyx, black olive, charcoal, and jet."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cc/Color_icon_black_new2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_black","wikidata":"Q7460340"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/EbonyLabelOfDen-BritishMuseum-August19-08.jpg/500px-EbonyLabelOfDen-BritishMuseum-August19-08.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/EbonyLabelOfDen-BritishMuseum-August19-08.jpg/1280px-EbonyLabelOfDen-BritishMuseum-August19-08.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/EbonyLabelOfDen-BritishMuseum-August19-08.jpg/500px-EbonyLabelOfDen-BritishMuseum-August19-08.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ebony label depicting the pharaoh Den, found in his tomb in Abydos, circa 3000 BC","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEbonyLabelOfDen-BritishMuseum-August19-08.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Ebony_elefant.JPG/500px-Ebony_elefant.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Ebony_elefant.JPG/500px-Ebony_elefant.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parse